{
  "dom_id": "step-q164523",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
